		<div class="post" id="post-2376213">
		<div class="long">
			<div class="head">
									<div class="options">
						<a href="javascript:;" onclick="togglePostFold(event,2376213)" class="btn btn-default btn-small btn-sm">Fold</a>
					</div>
								<div class="title" id="post-title-2376213">
																		
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agent-macleod" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1922529); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1922529&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="Agent MacLeod" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1922529)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/agent-macleod" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1922529); return false;" >Agent MacLeod</a></span> <span class="odate time_1442364467 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Sep 2015 00:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2376213">
									

<p>This is a non-fiction essay on a creative writing site describing artistic pieces <em>about</em> artistic pieces.</p>
<p>It's just turtles all the way down, folks.</p>
<p>With the level of meta that this piece invokes (side note: this is commentary on the above, which is just making this more and more meta. Seriously. Turtles all the way down.) established, I have to express two thoughts on this:</p>
<ol>
<li>Because an SCP item about art is, IRL, meta-art, it's easy to get caught in the AWCY? trap, depicting artists as snobby hipsters who drink PBR and make sculptures out of their own piss. It's <em>super</em> easy to do that. What's hard is creating an in-universe art piece, then making an SCP article (or other piece of fiction) about that art piece that is itself valid art IRL. The obvious route, to me, is to avoid it entirely. Make both the SCP entry and the object it describes art about the same thing.</li>
<li>AWCY?, as an in-universe group, is kind of pointless. Whether you headcanon them as an actual group or as simply an in-universe meme, something in-universe artists slap onto shit, it serves no real purpose. What does the question even mean? In situations where the answer is &quot;Yes, you created a cool art piece,&quot; the question is unnecessary (with one exception whose number I can't recall at the moment). When the answer is the much more common &quot;You aren't cool you will never be cool,&quot; all it shows is that the in-universe artist is a piss-sculpting PBR drinker.<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-636242-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-636242-1')">1</a></sup> We've got enough piss-sculptures on the site. We don't need more, because it's both redundant and, as Kalinin said in the essay, obnoxious as all hell.</li>
</ol>
<p>Good to see the King of Concrit back on his throne.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-636242-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-636242-1')">1</a>. I don't drink beer. If anyone actually <em>likes</em> Pabst, and doesn't drink it just because they want to be like the cool kids, I'm not talking about you. I wouldn't really know too much about it, TBH.</div>
</div>

							</div>

								
