					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/steeltitan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1871133); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1871133&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605818406" alt="Steeltitan" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1871133)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/steeltitan" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1871133); return false;" >Steeltitan</a></span> <span class="odate time_1442374945 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">16 Sep 2015 03:42</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2376312">
									

<p>The way I see it, the main reason that AWCY is such a prevalent group is precisely because of the things they sculpt and their art style. There might be hundreds of peaceful, more creative anartist people and groups, but AWCY is like a terrorist group. It might not represent the whole population, but people and associations notice it a lot more. A landscape painting that anomalously causes a sense of nostalgia, or a still-life of cheese that causes hunger would be great, but the Foundation probably wouldn't notice or care about it enough to send a containment team. Art bombs and 447 hijackings are much more likely to get a group on the foundation's watch list. The other groups might even have peace treaties or negotiations with the foundation to avoid containment, or just not show up on the GoI page, which usually deals with more public and interactive groups.</p>
<p>I would like to see a tale about that painting though. It sounds very nice, if rather difficult to pull off with the right feels. Perhaps relating to Kiryu labs? I remember those had very nice writing and style which would fit the idea of a nostalgia painting which isn't anomolous enough for actual SCP designation.</p>

							</div>

								
					
