			<div class="content" id="post-content-2376994">
									

<p>This.<br />
I agree with this.</p>
<p>This is a beautifully written metacritique and I can't wait to read more. As an art therapist and a trained fine artist, I admit a vested interest in making an artist representation less like the &quot;other&quot; and more like someone you would find in the grocery store or at the movies.</p>
<p>I however, wonder if the stereotypical depictions of terrorist artists are comfortable because they ARE so far removed from the idea of a familiar identity. The AWCY are doing things in the interest of themselves, not others&#8230;a feature alone that indicate a litany of issues that could be recalled by peering in the DSM-V . These are personalities that are hard to reconcile with emotionally and logically, or especially if you have empathy&#8230;and many artists do have the ability to exercise a degree of empathy.</p>
<p>I don't know where I'm going with this but this will be my go-to article when I contemplate what I do as an artist and more interestingly, why I do.</p>

							</div>

								
